WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.980
The Meta and Google implementation documents illustrate how designs can share an

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.800
Open Rack foundation without being identical products.

3
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.860
Read each document's scope and revision before comparing physical layout, support arrangements,

4
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.300
power distribution and integration assumptions.

5
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.520
The animation places the common interface contract beside implementation-specific choices.

6
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.340
It doesn't assert that all equipment or accessories transfer between the two.

7
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.240
A dimension from one implementation is not a universal O R V

8
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.900
three dimension, and a power arrangement from another doesn't become the only

9
00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:31.820
permitted architecture.

10
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.520
Hold the deployment requirements constant and ask how each proposed configuration meets

11
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:36.720
them.

12
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:41.160
One design may suit a particular service approach, equipment population or site

13
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:41.840
envelope better.

14
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.260
That isn't evidence that the other design is generally inferior.

15
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:51.160
Keep the comparison in an evidence matrix: requirement, proposed implementation, supporting source

16
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:52.600
and remaining qualification.

17
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.660
This method preserves useful examples without turning a vendor name or a

18
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.840
familiar drawing into an unsupported system-level conclusion.

